WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
Speaker 1: Last Friday, we got the new consumer&nbsp;
price index data and we found out that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:10.640
inflation rate year over year for the month of May&nbsp;
was 8.6%. There was this idea before the numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.400
were released that maybe inflation had peaked&nbsp;
over the past couple of months and we'd start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:19.760
head down a little bit. But this new data release&nbsp;
really throws a wrench into that theory because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:26.480
we're still trending upwards, albeit slightly. In&nbsp;
March, we were at 8.5% year over year inflation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:33.360
In April, we declined modestly to 8.3%.&nbsp;
And in May, once again, we went up to 8.6%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.800
It was predicted by economists ahead of the CPI&nbsp;
data release that inflation would go down to about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:45.440
8.2%. So instead of being slightly down, we're&nbsp;
heading slightly up. The Consumer Price Index,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:51.680
or CPI is a broad assessment of prices in the&nbsp;
economy, including everything from food to fuel to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:58.480
housing to discretionary products. The main reason&nbsp;
for the CPI increase appears to be that gas prices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:04.960
have gone up quite significantly over the past&nbsp;
month or so. Gas prices due to pent up demand in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:11.040
the economy, given the recovery from the pandemic&nbsp;
and due to Russia's invasion of Ukraine and other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:17.360
factors continue to go up, they just reached a&nbsp;
record $5 a gallon and it's likely a trend that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:22.480
going to continue all summer long because we know&nbsp;
that gas prices tend to go up with demand during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:26.880
the summer months. And certainly the situation&nbsp;
in Russia and Ukraine has not settled down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:34.240
So we may see $6 a gallon gasoline relatively&nbsp;
soon. Who knows? So it's going to be difficult,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:40.000
of course, to have CPI data going down when&nbsp;
you have gas prices going up so significantly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.520
If you're going to do that, you really have to&nbsp;
make up for it in the other categories that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.640
assesses. And we just did not do nearly enough&nbsp;
to make up for it. Interestingly enough, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:55.440
core inflation, which removes food and energy&nbsp;
from the index is at 6% for the month of May year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:00.720
over year, whereas in April is at 6.2%. So that's&nbsp;
positive. That's heading in the right direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.840
But some people will say, well, you can't just&nbsp;
really take out energy and food costs from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:10.560
equation, given that they're such an important&nbsp;
metric of what takes place in the economy. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:16.320
CPI release caused the markets to tumble quite&nbsp;
a bit at the end of last week. On Thursday and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:22.720
Friday we saw the major indices drop about three&nbsp;
4%. And the reason is that high inflation means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:26.720
that the Federal Reserve is probably going&nbsp;
to have to be more hawkish when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:31.840
raising interest rates, how often they're going&nbsp;
to raise the interest rates and by how much?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:37.120
Because the idea is if you have high inflation,&nbsp;
you have to cool down the economy to encourage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:42.320
less spending. And that means that companies&nbsp;
won't be able to make as much profit, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:46.960
means that investors won't make as much money,&nbsp;
which means that people often will sell off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:52.000
Aside from raising the interest rates, which is&nbsp;
really under the purview of the Federal Reserve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.720
there's not much that the Biden administration&nbsp;
can do when it comes to inflation and gas prices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:01.120
That's just the sad reality in all of this. When&nbsp;
it comes to gas prices. We know that he can take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:07.440
more out from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, but&nbsp;
that maybe will save you $0.10 here, $0.20 here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:14.800
It's not going to be enough to significantly drive&nbsp;
costs down. And we also know that the Strategic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:19.440
Petroleum Reserve really is meant for emergencies.&nbsp;
So if there was a serious emergency that came our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.800
way and we had just released a bunch of oil and&nbsp;
gas, that may not be the best thing to do. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:29.920
about every country we know is dealing with the&nbsp;
high gas prices and the high inflation. So for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:35.680
me personally, I have a tough time blaming Joe&nbsp;
Biden for this. The people who think that Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:39.200
and Republicans would do better. I'd like&nbsp;
to hear how they would go about doing that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:43.360
I think there's just a sentiment of, well, with&nbsp;
Donald Trump and the Republicans were in charge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:47.600
We didn't have high inflation, we didn't have gas&nbsp;
prices. So clearly, if we put them back in charge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:51.920
they'd be able to get a handle on it. But that's&nbsp;
really just not how it works. Unfortunately, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:56.720
average voter doesn't know about that. They don't&nbsp;
know that presidents have very little control over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:02.720
inflation and gas prices. So it's going to&nbsp;
be tough to communicate that to the American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:06.640
people ahead of the midterms, especially when&nbsp;
these really are kitchen table issues. Can we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:10.800
afford gas? Can we afford these other things?&nbsp;
Because prices everywhere are going up. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:15.360
it's a big challenge for Democrats heading into&nbsp;
the midterms. We definitely want to focus on gun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:19.440
control and abortion because we're thinking more&nbsp;
and more. Those could be winning issues for us in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.320
November. We also have to effectively&nbsp;
address how we're going to try to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:29.120
a handle on inflation and gas prices, even&nbsp;
if it's just doing whatever we can. Right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.720
to make some sort of effort to appeal to&nbsp;
people to vote for us again. So that's. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:37.600
latest. When it comes to inflation, gas prices&nbsp;
doesn't seem like we have it under control yet.

